{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Say to the priests, the sons of Aaron, and you shall say to them.</b> But do we not yet know that the priests are the sons of Aaron? What does the phrase בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן come to teach? Moreover, the doubling of the utterance requires explanation. And what seems close to me to say on this is that Aaron, the holy one of HaShem, was the source of holiness, and from him holiness was drawn also to his sons — but they were not like him in every respect, for they were distant from the source of holiness. Therefore Aaron, who was the source of holiness, was not permitted to defile himself for any relative, even for father or mother; and likewise every High Priest of his seed resembles him in every respect, for he receives the influx of holiness directly and at first hand from the primal holiness, blessed be He, as it is written: ״כִּי נֵזֶר שֶׁמֶן מִשְׁחַת אֱלֹהָיו עָלָיו״ — נֵזֶר in the sense of separation, meaning that he was separated from the anointing oil of his Elohim and it was placed upon him not by way of an intermediary. But the sons of Aaron, who received holiness through the intermediacy of Aaron — this is treated from two sides: for on their own there is no holiness in them, and by virtue of being sons of Aaron there is holiness in them. Therefore their law is divided: from the aspect of the holiness they received, they may not defile themselves to distant dead; and from the aspect that they are somewhat distant from the source of holiness, it was permitted to them to defile themselves for close relatives. Therefore he doubled the utterance: the first utterance, ״אֱמֹר אֶל הַכֹּהֲנִים בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן״ — from the aspect of their being sons of Aaron, therefore holiness adheres to them through the intermediacy of Aaron, therefore it is fitting to command them ״לְנֶפֶשׁ לֹא יִטַּמָּא בְּעַמָּיו״. ״וְאָמַרְתָּ אֲלֵהֶם״ — this is the second utterance, from the aspect of their own essence, that in their own essence there is not such abundant holiness — for this is alluded to in the word אֲלֵהֶם, as if they were not sons of Aaron — and consequently it is fitting to command them the command ״כִּי אִם לִשְׁאֵרוֹ״, that they are permitted to defile themselves for close relatives." "Another matter — the very opposite of this: to say to them the first utterance, that holiness adheres to them by virtue of their being sons of Aaron, who was the source of holiness. ״וְאָמַרְתָּ אֲלֵהֶם״ — from their own aspect: that even if they were not sons of Aaron, in any case, since they are the ministers of the living El, it is not fitting for them to defile themselves ״כִּי אִם לִשְׁאֵרוֹ הַקָּרוֹב אֵלָיו״, for it is enough for the servant to be as his master — for HaShem, blessed be He, also occupies Himself with His close ones, as it is written (Deuteronomy 34:6): ״וַיִּקְבֹּר אֹתוֹ בַגַּי״, and these are the close ones of HaShem, blessed be He."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:45.345681891Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}